window.BUNDLED_INTERVIEW = {"data":"","translations":{"scenic":{"category":"scenic","items":[{"number":1,"translatedQuestion":"How was the Danxia landform in Shaoguan formed?","translatedAnswer":"Geological studies show that 25 million years ago this area was a low-lying lake. Later, movements of the Earth\u0027s crust raised the land and the lake drained away, forming towering peaks. The sediments on the lakebed oxidized and turned into red rock."},{"number":2,"translatedQuestion":"Who named this red sandstone landform the Danxia landform?","translatedAnswer":"In the 1930s, Chen Guoda, a professor at Sun Yat-sen University, conducted in-depth studies of Danxia Mountain and the red-rock mountains of South China and named this type of red sandstone terrain the Danxia landform. The name was soon accepted and adopted by the academic community. Since then, landforms around the world formed of red conglomerate and characterized by sheer red cliffs have been called Danxia landforms."},{"number":3,"translatedQuestion":"Why are the peaks along the Jinjiang River called “A Herd of Elephants Crossing the River”?","translatedAnswer":"The peaks look like a herd of elephants about to cross the Jinjiang River and come toward us. Their trunks, tusks, ears, and eyes are all vividly recognizable, so the scene is called “A Herd of Elephants Crossing the River.”"},{"number":4,"translatedQuestion":"Why is the distant mountain to your right, as viewed from Yangyuan Bridge, called “Jade Maiden Blocking the River”?","translatedAnswer":"Look at the mountain in the distance to your right. It resembles a sleeping young woman; from right to left, you can make out her head, neck, chest, and abdomen, with a clearly defined outline. That is why the scene is called “Sleeping Beauty” or “Jade Maiden Blocking the River.”"},{"number":5,"translatedQuestion":"Please tell us about Yangyuan Mountain at Shaoguan\u0027s Danxia Mountain.","translatedAnswer":"This is Yangyuan Mountain, or “the male mountain.” This pillar of rock, called Yangyuan Stone, is 28 meters high and 7 meters in diameter and closely resembles a phallus; it is the reason for the mountain\u0027s name. Experts believe that Yangyuan Stone was originally part of the mountain\u0027s cliff face, from which the forces of nature separated and sculpted it into its present shape over 300,000 years ago. In the past, people prayed for male descendants to flourish and for their family line to continue, so the stone was often worshipped."},{"number":6,"translatedQuestion":"What honors and titles have been bestowed on the Kaiping Diaolou?","translatedAnswer":"The Kaiping Diaolou are acclaimed as “a model of overseas Chinese culture” and “a museum of world architectural art.” They have been designated a National Key Cultural Relic Protection Site by the State Council. At their peak, there were 3,300 towers; today, 1,833 are officially registered. The site has been designated a National Key Cultural Relic Protection Site by the State Council, and 20 of the better-preserved towers have been inscribed on the UNESCO World Heritage List."},{"number":7,"translatedQuestion":"What are the three main types of Kaiping Diaolou?","translatedAnswer":"These buildings fall into three types: residential towers for individual families; communal towers built with contributions from villagers; and watchtowers, used mainly to keep watch and guard against bandits."},{"number":8,"translatedQuestion":"Please describe the structure and decoration of the diaolou in Zili Village.","translatedAnswer":"The most exquisitely decorated tower is Mingshi Tower, a reinforced-concrete residence built in 1925. It is five stories high. At the center of the roof is a hexagonal lookout pavilion combining Chinese and Western styles, and each corner of the fifth floor has a corner turret, also known as a “swallow\u0027s nest.” The tower is large and imposing, with a heavy iron door and sturdy iron windows. Its interior is sumptuously furnished and has complete living facilities."},{"number":9,"translatedQuestion":"Why did Xie Weili, a Chinese American, name his garden Li Garden?","translatedAnswer":"The garden\u0027s name, Li, comes from its owner\u0027s name and expresses the meaning of the Chinese idiom xiushen liben (修身立本), which is inscribed on an archway in the garden. It means that self-cultivation is the foundation for conducting oneself and establishing a family. The owner\u0027s philosophy is also reflected in many other inscriptions and couplets in the garden."},{"number":10,"translatedQuestion":"Why do we say that the residential buildings in Li Garden combine Chinese and Western architectural elements?","translatedAnswer":"The villa roofs follow the style of Chinese palatial architecture, while the building exteriors are Western in style. The staircases, floors, and wall decorations inside also combine Chinese and Western elements, including Western fireplaces and chandeliers, Italian tiles, Chinese rosewood furniture, murals depicting folk tales, and gilded woodcarvings."},{"number":11,"translatedQuestion":"Why did Sun Yat-sen give up hope in the Qing government and decide to overthrow it?","translatedAnswer":"China\u0027s defeats at the hands of foreign invaders, together with the Qing government\u0027s corruption and incompetence, intensified his patriotic indignation. He concluded that the Qing court was rotten to the core and had to be overthrown and replaced by a democratic republic."},{"number":12,"translatedQuestion":"When and where did Sun Yat-sen establish China\u0027s first political party?","translatedAnswer":"In 1905, Sun Yat-sen went to Japan and founded China\u0027s first political party there, the Chinese Revolutionary Alliance (Tongmenghui). It later developed into the Kuomintang."},{"number":13,"translatedQuestion":"Please briefly introduce the designer of Sun Yat-sen Memorial Hall.","translatedAnswer":"Sun Yat-sen Memorial Hall was designed by Lu Yanzhi, an outstanding architect in modern China. Born in Tianjin, Lu graduated from Tsinghua University in Beijing and later went to Cornell University in the United States to study architecture. He died of lung cancer in 1929, before the memorial hall was completed, at just 36 years of age."},{"number":14,"translatedQuestion":"What are the distinctive features of Sun Yat-sen Memorial Hall\u0027s appearance and construction?","translatedAnswer":"Sun Yat-sen Memorial Hall is an octagonal, palace-style reinforced-concrete building. Its exterior follows the style of traditional Chinese architecture, while its structure employed the most advanced building technology of the time. The main building is 58 meters high and has a floor area of 12,000 square meters."},{"number":15,"translatedQuestion":"What modern architectural techniques did the designer use to build Sun Yat-sen Memorial Hall?","translatedAnswer":"Thanks to the designer\u0027s ingenious plan, the auditorium has excellent acoustics and the audience\u0027s view is unobstructed by columns. The eight massive columns supporting the four large-span steel trusses—which carry the huge domed roof—are cleverly concealed inside the walls."},{"number":16,"translatedQuestion":"Please introduce the occupant of the Nanyue King Tomb.","translatedAnswer":"The tomb\u0027s occupant, Zhao Mo, was the second king of Nanyue. He succeeded to the throne in the fourth year of Emperor Wu of Han\u0027s Jianyuan era, took the title Emperor Wen, and reigned for 16 years (137–122 BCE)."},{"number":17,"translatedQuestion":"Where did archaeologists excavate the remains of the Nanyue Kingdom palace?","translatedAnswer":"Archaeologists carried out a trial excavation east of the former Children\u0027s Park in Guangzhou and uncovered about 500 square meters of the Nanyue Kingdom palace site."},{"number":18,"translatedQuestion":"Which gold seal was the first imperial seal unearthed through archaeological excavation in China?","translatedAnswer":"The gold seal inscribed “Wendi Xingxi” unearthed from the Nanyue King Tomb is the only surviving seal of a Qin or Han emperor to have been recovered in an archaeological excavation in China."},{"number":19,"translatedQuestion":"What does the inscribed bronze dagger-axe unearthed from the Nanyue King Tomb tell us about its background?","translatedAnswer":"Among the weapons unearthed from the Nanyue King Tomb, one bronze dagger-axe bears the inscription “Chancellor Zhang Yi, fourth year of the King” (王四年相邦张义). This indicates that it was made under Zhang Yi\u0027s supervision during the reign of King Hui of Qin and brought from Qin to Nanyue."},{"number":20,"translatedQuestion":"How many people were buried as sacrificial victims in the Nanyue King Tomb? Who was in the front chamber, and who was in the outer coffin chamber?","translatedAnswer":"A total of 15 people buried as sacrificial victims were found in the tomb. One was in the front chamber and held the title Jingxiangling (景巷令); another was in the outer coffin chamber and may have been a charioteer."},{"number":21,"translatedQuestion":"What functions did Chen Clan Ancestral Hall serve?","translatedAnswer":"The Chen Clan Ancestral Hall was used to worship the clan\u0027s common ancestor and as a venue for clan activities on festive occasions and at other times. Also known as Chen Clan Academy, it served as a school where young members of the Chen clan studied."},{"number":22,"translatedQuestion":"Why is Chen Clan Ancestral Hall acclaimed as the “Pearl of Lingnan Architectural Art”?","translatedAnswer":"In its architectural ornamentation, Chen Clan Ancestral Hall brings together the finest of Guangdong\u0027s folk crafts and is acclaimed as the “Pearl of Lingnan Architectural Art.” It was therefore converted into the Guangdong Folk Arts Museum in 1959."},{"number":23,"translatedQuestion":"Please describe the stone drums on either side of the main entrance to Chen Clan Ancestral Hall.","translatedAnswer":"The stone drums on either side of the main entrance symbolize the family\u0027s status. In old feudal China, a family could place large drums in front of its residence only if one of its members had achieved the rank of jinshi or higher in the imperial examinations."},{"number":24,"translatedQuestion":"Please explain the “fortune has arrived” image at Chen Clan Ancestral Hall (the “fortune” character on the reverse side).","translatedAnswer":"This is also the Chinese character for “fortune,” shown reversed from left to right. In Chinese, the word for “inverted” (倒, dào) is a homophone of the word for “arrived” (到, dào), so an inverted fortune character represents “fortune has arrived.”"},{"number":25,"translatedQuestion":"Please explain the Lingnan fruit offerings at Chen Clan Ancestral Hall.","translatedAnswer":"Various prized fruits from Lingnan are carved at the tops of the balusters on the platform, including peaches, star fruit, papaya, and lychees. These are symbolic offerings that members of the Chen clan presented to their ancestors."}]},"guide":{"category":"guide","items":[{"number":1,"translatedQuestion":"To avoid meeting the wrong group, missing a group, or failing to meet a group at all, what information should a local guide clarify when reading the tour reception plan?","translatedAnswer":"The local guide should pay attention to the following:\n(1) The names and telephone numbers of the domestic tour operator\u0027s contact person and the national guide.\n(2) The number of group members and their sex, names, and occupations.\n(3) The service number, arrival time, and meeting point for the transport by which the group will arrive locally.\n(4) For an inbound group, also learn the name of the tour operator in the place of origin and the tour leader; the group\u0027s foreign-language name or code; the visitors\u0027 nationalities, languages, and religious beliefs; and the group\u0027s entry and exit points."},{"number":2,"translatedQuestion":"What aspects of transportation tickets should a local guide check when receiving an inbound group?","translatedAnswer":"(1) Whether the group\u0027s transportation tickets to the next destination have been booked as planned, whether there have been any changes, and whether revised arrangements have been confirmed.\n(2) Whether the group has return tickets.\n(3) Whether international air tickets have been issued for domestic flights within the country.\n(4) Whether the group\u0027s return air tickets have been reconfirmed."},{"number":3,"translatedQuestion":"How should a local guide prepare in terms of language and knowledge before joining a tour group?","translatedAnswer":"(1) Prepare commentary for the sightseeing and visits specified in the reception plan; foreign-language guides should also prepare how they will express it in the target language.\n(2) For groups with specialized interests, prepare the relevant professional knowledge and vocabulary.\n(3) Prepare to discuss current topics, major domestic and international news, and subjects that may interest visitors.\n(4) Make detailed inquiries in advance about any attractions with which you are unfamiliar."},{"number":4,"translatedQuestion":"When meeting an inbound group at an airport or railway station, how should a local guide verify that it is the group they are assigned to receive?","translatedAnswer":"(1) Ask which country or region the group has come from, the names of the tour operators in the place of origin and in China, and the names of the tour leader and national guide.\n(2) If the group has neither a tour leader nor a national guide, check the group name and number, country of origin, number and names of visitors, and the main itinerary with the group members."},{"number":5,"translatedQuestion":"As the local guide, deliver a standard welcome speech to the tour group.","translatedAnswer":"The speech should include:\n(1) Welcoming the visitors on behalf of the local receiving travel service, the driver, and yourself.\n(2) Introducing yourself and your organization.\n(3) Introducing the driver.\n(4) Sincerely expressing your wish to provide high-quality service.\n(5) Wishing the visitors an enjoyable trip.\n\nSample welcome speech (for reference only):\nLadies and gentlemen, good morning!\nWelcome to Guangzhou. My name is xxx, and our driver\u0027s name is xxx. I am a guide with the Guangzhou branch of China International Travel Service. We are honored to be your guides during your stay (or: We are very pleased to be with you while you visit our city). I expect that for most of you, this is your first visit to Guangzhou. We will do our best to make your stay here enjoyable, relaxing, and comfortable. If you are dissatisfied with anything about our work or the hotel service, please do not hesitate to tell us so that we can make improvements promptly. We look forward to your cooperation. I hope you enjoy your sightseeing here and have a wonderful time."},{"number":6,"translatedQuestion":"What should a local guide include when introducing the local area to visitors?","translatedAnswer":"(1) The introduction should cover the local climate, population, administrative divisions, social life, cultural traditions, local specialties, and history.\n(2) At appropriate times, also describe the cityscape, its development, and important buildings, streets, and other features along the route."},{"number":7,"translatedQuestion":"After visitors have checked into the hotel, how should a local guide stay in contact with the tour group?","translatedAnswer":"The local guide should note the room numbers of the tour leader, national guide, and group members, and give the national guide and tour leader ways to get in touch, such as the guide\u0027s room number and telephone number, so they can make contact promptly if needed."},{"number":8,"translatedQuestion":"If the tour leader or national guide proposes a minor change to the reception plan or asks to add a new sightseeing activity, how should the local guide respond?","translatedAnswer":"(1) Report the request promptly to the relevant department of the travel agency. Accommodate requests that are reasonable and feasible as far as possible.\n(2) For activities that require an additional charge, explain this to the tour leader or visitors in advance and collect the fee in accordance with the applicable rules.\n(3) If a request truly cannot be accommodated, explain the reasons fully and patiently."},{"number":9,"translatedQuestion":"When a tour group with checked baggage arrives locally, how should the local guide check the baggage?","translatedAnswer":"(1) Help the group gather its baggage in the designated area and remind visitors to check that their belongings are complete and undamaged.\n(2) After checking the baggage with the tour leader and national guide, hand it over to the baggage handler and complete the handover formalities together.\n(3) If any baggage is missing or damaged, help the affected visitor file a report for lost baggage or compensation with the relevant transportation provider."},{"number":10,"translatedQuestion":"Before visitors get off the vehicle to visit an attraction, what information should the local guide give them?","translatedAnswer":"The local guide should explain and remind visitors to remember the tour vehicle\u0027s type, color, identifying mark, and registration number, as well as where it is parked and when it will depart."},{"number":11,"translatedQuestion":"What should a local guide do regarding the tour leader\u0027s and visitors\u0027 travel documents?","translatedAnswer":"(1) In general, the local guide should not keep the group\u0027s travel documents. If a visitor\u0027s document is needed to complete a procedure, return it immediately once it has been used.\n(2) The day before departure from the destination, check your own belongings to make sure you have not retained any visitors\u0027 documents or tickets. If you have, return them immediately and verify them in person.\n(3) Some hotels require the tour leader to leave their documents at the front desk when the group checks in. When the group checks out, remind the tour leader to retrieve them.\n(4) Before an inbound group departs the country, remind visitors to have their passports and declaration forms ready for inspection by border control and customs."},{"number":12,"translatedQuestion":"How should tour guides prevent visitors from getting separated from the group?","translatedAnswer":"(1) Remind visitors to remember the name of the local receiving travel service, the vehicle number, and the hotel name; not to wander too far from the group while it is moving; and not to return to the hotel too late after staying out or going out on their own.\n(2) During visits and sightseeing, regularly brief visitors on the itinerary and clearly tell them the meal times and locations, sightseeing route, meeting time and place, and parking location.\n(3) Stay with the visitors and count the group frequently.\n(4) The local guide, national guide, and tour leader should coordinate closely: the local guide leads at the front, while the national guide and tour leader bring up the rear.\n(5) The local guide should hold visitors\u0027 attention with engaging commentary."},{"number":13,"translatedQuestion":"When a tour group checks into a hotel, what should the local guide explain to the visitors?","translatedAnswer":"The local guide should tell the group where to find facilities such as the currency exchange, Chinese and Western restaurants, entertainment venues, shops, and public restrooms, and explain important points about checking in and staying at the hotel."},{"number":14,"translatedQuestion":"What should a local guide do before, during, and after a meal for a tour group?","translatedAnswer":"(1) Before the meal, verify and confirm each detail with the restaurant, including the location, time, number of diners, meal standard, and any special requirements.\n(2) During the meal, check on the group once or twice, answer visitors\u0027 questions, and supervise and assess the restaurant\u0027s service quality.\n(3) After the meal, settle the bill with the restaurant strictly according to the actual number of diners and the agreed meal standard."},{"number":15,"translatedQuestion":"How should a local guide arrange the tour group\u0027s scheduled and optional entertainment activities?","translatedAnswer":"(1) If an entertainment program is included in the itinerary, the local guide should accompany the group and introduce the program.\n(2) For an optional activity not included in the itinerary, such as a performance or other entertainment that visitors wish to attend at their own expense, the local guide should generally assist but usually does not accompany them.\n(3) If visitors are going to a large entertainment venue, remind them not to get separated and pay attention to their whereabouts and surroundings to prevent problems."},{"number":16,"translatedQuestion":"What should a local guide do before a tour group leaves its hotel?","translatedAnswer":"(1) Gather and check baggage for dispatch. If the group has baggage to be checked, at the agreed time count it with the tour leader, national guide, and baggage handler, and inspect how it has been packed.\n(2) Complete the hotel check-out. Unless there are special circumstances, the local guide should check out by noon.\n(3) Assemble the group and board the vehicle. Before leaving, ask visitors whether they have settled their accounts with the hotel and ask them to return their room keys to the front desk. Once everyone is seated, carefully count the people present and remind visitors to check that they have not left anything behind."},{"number":17,"translatedQuestion":"What should a local guide do on the way back to the hotel after a day\u0027s sightseeing?","translatedAnswer":"(1) On the way back, review the day\u0027s visits and sightseeing; provide additional commentary if needed and answer visitors\u0027 questions.\n(2) If the vehicle is taking a different route back to the hotel, give commentary on the scenery along the way.\n(3) Before visitors get off, announce the schedule for that evening or the following day, including the departure time and meeting place.\n(4) Remind visitors to take their personal belongings with them.\n(5) The local guide should get off first, assist visitors as they leave the vehicle, and say goodbye."},{"number":18,"translatedQuestion":"When a tour group enters its destination country, what are the three border checks it must go through?","translatedAnswer":"The three checks required for entry are health and quarantine inspection, travel-document inspection, and customs inspection."},{"number":19,"translatedQuestion":"To avoid a missed flight, train, or ship, what timing should a guide allow when taking a tour group to its departure point?","translatedAnswer":"The guide should ensure that the group reaches the airport, railway station, or port before the flight, train, or ship departs. The specific requirements are:\n(1) For an international flight or a flight to a coastal city, arrive 2 hours in advance.\n(2) For a domestic flight, arrive 1.5 hours in advance.\n(3) For a train or ship, arrive 1 hour in advance."},{"number":20,"translatedQuestion":"What principles should a national guide follow when agreeing on the itinerary with the local guide?","translatedAnswer":"(1) If the local guide revises the itinerary, the national guide should maintain the principle that the order of activities may be changed, but activities may not be omitted. Report the matter to the originating travel agency if necessary.\n(2) If the local guide recommends optional activities at the visitors\u0027 own expense, the national guide should seek the views of all group members."},{"number":21,"translatedQuestion":"What should a national guide do at each destination on the tour?","translatedAnswer":"(1) Brief the local guide on the tour group\u0027s situation and assist the local guide.\n(2) Monitor service quality at each destination and, as appropriate, offer suggestions and recommendations for improvement.\n(3) Protect visitors\u0027 safety and prevent and handle various incidents.\n(4) Advise visitors on shopping.\n(5) Stay in contact with the next destination."},{"number":22,"translatedQuestion":"What role does a national guide play during a tour, and what responsibilities does the guide have?","translatedAnswer":"The national guide represents the originating travel agency and should take part in the group\u0027s activities from beginning to end. The guide is responsible for coordinating every stage of the itinerary, supervising implementation of the reception plan, and coordinating the tour leader, local guide, driver, and other reception staff."},{"number":23,"translatedQuestion":"How should a national guide supervise and advise the group while it is shopping?","translatedAnswer":"(1) If the local guide schedules too many shopping stops or extends shopping time, the national guide should raise the matter promptly.\n(2) Remind visitors to pay attention to the quality and price of goods and to beware of counterfeit or inferior products being passed off as genuine or high-quality goods.\n(3) When visitors in an inbound group buy expensive items, especially cultural relics, remind them to keep their receipts for inspection when leaving the country. When they buy Chinese patent medicines or Chinese medicinal materials, explain the relevant Chinese customs regulations."},{"number":24,"translatedQuestion":"What should a national guide do when a tour group arrives at a hotel?","translatedAnswer":"(1) Take the initiative in helping the tour leader complete the group\u0027s check-in.\n(2) Ask the tour leader to allocate the rooms, keep a list of the room assignments, and exchange room numbers with the tour leader for contact purposes.\n(3) Warmly guide visitors to their rooms.\n(4) If the local guide is not staying at the hotel, the national guide should take full responsibility for looking after the group.\n(5) Note the hotel\u0027s front-desk telephone number and how to contact the local guide in case they are needed."},{"number":25,"translatedQuestion":"What measures should a national guide take to monitor service quality at each destination?","translatedAnswer":"(1) If the activities at a destination substantially repeat those at previous stops, suggest that the local guide make the necessary adjustments.\n(2) If there are concerns about the local reception service—for example, accommodation, meals, or vehicles do not meet the standards in the original agreement; the local guide unilaterally reduces or cancels sightseeing activities, adds paid activities, or schedules too much shopping—raise them with the local guide in a constructive manner.\n(3) If the service still does not improve, raise the matter directly with the receiving travel service and, if necessary, report it to the originating travel agency."},{"number":26,"translatedQuestion":"What should a national guide do when an inbound group is about to leave the country?","translatedAnswer":"(1) At the end of the tour, remind visitors to take their belongings and travel documents.\n(2) Ask visitors for their opinions and suggestions about the reception service as a whole.\n(3) Give a farewell speech, thank the visitors for their cooperation, and welcome them to visit again."},{"number":27,"translatedQuestion":"As the national guide, deliver a farewell speech to the tour group.","translatedAnswer":"The speech should include:\n(1) Looking back on the tour and thanking everyone for their cooperation.\n(2) Expressing friendship and the sadness of parting.\n(3) Sincerely asking visitors for their opinions and suggestions about the reception service.\n(4) If anything went wrong during the tour or any aspect of the service fell short of expectations, taking this opportunity to apologize to the visitors again.\n(5) Offering warm wishes.\n\nSample farewell speech (for reference only):\nLadies and gentlemen,\nYour visit to China is now coming to an end. Over the past 10 days, you have climbed the Great Wall, one of the Eight Wonders of the World, which is said to have been the only man-made object on Earth visible from the moon. You have visited the Forbidden City in Beijing, home to the emperors of the Ming and Qing dynasties; and you have reviewed the ranks of the First Emperor of Qin in Xi\u0027an—I mean the more than 6,000 Terracotta Warriors dating back over 2,000 years. The beautiful karst scenery of Guilin has delighted your eyes, and you have just tasted some of the best Chinese food in Guangzhou.... In short, I think you now have some idea of what China is like. But China is a vast country, with an area of 9.6 million square kilometers, a population of 1.3 billion, and a history spanning nearly 5,000 years. A 10-day visit to China is like the Chinese saying, “a dragonfly skimming the water”: it can only give you a brief glimpse. So, if you can come again, you will gain a better understanding of this country. I look forward to seeing you in China again in the near future.\n\nHowever, I know it is time for you to go home and be reunited with your families. As you prepare to leave, I want to say that I have had the pleasure of hosting a wonderful group of wonderful people. It has been my honor to spend the past 10 days with you. I know this trip could not satisfy every one of you, and there is much room for improvement in our work. We would therefore be very grateful for any comments or complaints you may have about our service.\n\nFriends, you have been such a wonderful group, and I really do not want to say goodbye. So all I can say is, “See you again!” Take care, and I wish you a pleasant journey home!"},{"number":28,"translatedQuestion":"How should a tour guide respond to visitors who are demanding or critical?","translatedAnswer":"(1) Listen carefully; do not accuse visitors of making unreasonable demands or offer hasty explanations before they have finished speaking.\n(2) Respond with a smile; do not show irritation or answer rudely just because you hear something unpleasant.\n(3) Explain patiently and factually when a reasonable request cannot be fulfilled. Do not reject it outright by simply saying, “It can\u0027t be done.”"},{"number":29,"translatedQuestion":"What kind of relationship should a tour guide maintain with visitors throughout a tour?","translatedAnswer":"(1) The guide should remember that they are the host: be warm and hospitable, show generosity, and be accommodating toward visitors.\n(2) The guide should not become overly familiar with visitors, get involved in disputes within the group, or stir up discord among visitors. Treat everyone equally and do not show favoritism."},{"number":30,"translatedQuestion":"How should a local guide assist with and arrange meetings between inbound visitors and other people?","translatedAnswer":"(1) If visitors—especially a professional tour group—meet with Chinese counterparts or officials, the guide may act as an interpreter when necessary. If another interpreter is present, the guide may listen without taking part.\n(2) If visitors wish to meet relatives or friends living in China, the guide should help arrange the meeting. In general, the guide is not obliged to interpret."},{"number":31,"translatedQuestion":"How should a local guide confirm the tour vehicle before receiving a group?","translatedAnswer":"(1) Contact the motor-transport company to confirm the vehicle type, registration number, and driver\u0027s name and telephone number.\n(2) Agree with the driver on the meeting point and provide the itinerary and specific schedule."},{"number":32,"translatedQuestion":"What items should a local guide prepare before joining a tour group?","translatedAnswer":"Before joining the group, obtain enough city maps, admission tickets, meal settlement slips, and funds according to the group size, and bring all necessary items: the reception plan, guide\u0027s IC card, the travel service\u0027s badge, guide flag, group-identification sign, and so on."},{"number":33,"translatedQuestion":"What should a local guide do while the tour group is traveling to an attraction?","translatedAnswer":"(1) Review the day\u0027s schedule.\n(2) Provide commentary on the scenery along the way.\n(3) Briefly introduce the attraction."},{"number":34,"translatedQuestion":"What should a local guide explain to visitors in front of an attraction map?","translatedAnswer":"(1) Explain the sightseeing route, how long it will take, and the meeting time and place.\n(2) Explain relevant points visitors should observe during the visit."},{"number":35,"translatedQuestion":"What should a local guide tell visitors at a meal featuring local specialties?","translatedAnswer":"The local guide should introduce the ingredients and preparation of the specialty dishes, their related stories or anecdotes, and how to eat them."},{"number":36,"translatedQuestion":"What should a national guide do if a conflict arises between the tour leader and visitors?","translatedAnswer":"(1) The national guide should not probe into the origins of their conflict.\n(2) The national guide should not express an opinion or become involved in the dispute.\n(3) If necessary, the national guide may help mediate their relationship."},{"number":37,"translatedQuestion":"During sightseeing, what should a national guide do to protect visitors\u0027 safety and prevent accidents?","translatedAnswer":"(1) During sightseeing, observe the surroundings and keep an eye on visitors\u0027 movements. Assist the local guide in completing the commentary, and prevent visitors from getting separated or having an accident.\n(2) Remind visitors to take care of their personal safety and belongings. If an unexpected incident occurs, rely on the local guide to handle it appropriately."},{"number":38,"translatedQuestion":"What should a national guide do when the group is traveling a long distance by plane, train, or ship?","translatedAnswer":"(1) When traveling by plane, train, or ship, actively seek the cooperation of staff from the civil aviation, railway, or shipping authorities to jointly provide security and onboard services.\n(2) During the journey, remind visitors to take care of their personal safety and belongings.\n(3) Organize entertainment and look after visitors\u0027 meals and rest.\n(4) Keep baggage claim checks and plane, train, or ship tickets and other documents safe, and hand them to the local guide upon arrival at the next stop.\n(5) When traveling by train, ask the tour leader in advance to assign compartments and sleeping berths."},{"number":39,"translatedQuestion":"What should a national guide do when the tour group leaves each destination?","translatedAnswer":"(1) Remind the local guide in advance to confirm the departure tickets and the exact departure time.\n(2) Assist the tour leader and local guide with departure arrangements.\n(3) Keep the tickets and travel documents safe."},{"number":40,"translatedQuestion":"What mental preparation should a tour guide make before joining a tour group?","translatedAnswer":"(1) Be prepared for demanding and complicated work. A tour guide must not only provide warm service in accordance with normal procedures, but also be mentally prepared to serve visitors with special needs and to face and handle problems or incidents that arise during reception.\n(2) Be prepared to face complaints and criticism. Guiding work is complicated and arduous. Sometimes, even after doing everything possible to serve a group warmly and attentively, a guide may still face visitors who are critical, complain, or find fault with the guide\u0027s work, and may even file a complaint. Guides should be psychologically prepared for this, remain calm and composed, and continue serving visitors willingly and without resentment."}]},"special":{"category":"special","items":[{"number":1,"translatedQuestion":"While a tour group is sightseeing at an attraction, one of the older tourists is discovered to be missing. What should the tour guide do?","translatedAnswer":"(1) Find out what happened and search promptly. Usually, the national guide and tour leader search separately while the local guide continues the tour with the other tourists.\n(2) Seek help from the local police station and the attraction’s management.\n(3) Contact the hotel to ask whether the tourist has returned there.\n(4) Report the incident to the travel agency.\n(5) Take care of the follow-up arrangements.\n(6) Write an incident report."},{"number":2,"translatedQuestion":"A tourist in the group is found to have an infectious disease. How should the tour guide handle the situation?","translatedAnswer":"(1) Find out in detail what the tourist has reported and establish the patient’s health condition.\n(2) Report the matter to the travel agency’s management and the local epidemic-prevention and health-quarantine authorities.\n(3) If the authorities consider an examination necessary, obtain the agreement of the tour leader and the patient, then take the patient to a local epidemic-prevention station for a serum test and examination.\n(4) If the patient is confirmed to have an infectious disease, follow the authorities’ advice on isolation or other measures.\n(5) If the patient is a visitor from abroad, explain to the patient and tour leader that, under the regulations in China, the patient must leave the country early, and help complete the necessary procedures.\n(6) Submit a written report to the relevant parties detailing the circumstances and how the matter was handled."},{"number":3,"translatedQuestion":"A tourist in the group suffers a heart attack. How should the local guide respond?","translatedAnswer":"(1) If it is confirmed to be a heart attack, have the patient lie flat where they are, with their head slightly raised. Ask a family member to retrieve the patient’s emergency medication and give it to them to help ease the condition.\n(2) Call an ambulance immediately or take the patient to the nearest hospital.\n(3) Notify the travel agency and ask it to send someone to assist.\n(4) While the hospital is treating the patient, ask a family member to remain there.\n(5) The local guide should visit the hospital from time to time. If the patient is a member of an inbound tour group, help arrange a separate visa and book return transportation to the patient’s home country, among other necessary arrangements.\n(6) Arrange activities for the rest of the group."},{"number":4,"translatedQuestion":"During free time, a tourist in the group is robbed by criminals. What should you do as the group’s tour guide?","translatedAnswer":"(1) Report the incident to the police immediately by calling 110. Give the time, location, and circumstances of the incident; describe the suspect or suspects; and provide the victim’s name and group number, along with the names, models, and identifying features of the stolen property.\n(2) Report the incident to the travel agency promptly and ask for instructions.\n(3) Reassure the tourists.\n(4) Write a report stating the nature of the case, the measures taken, and the victim’s response and requests.\n(5) Assist the relevant authorities with follow-up arrangements."},{"number":5,"translatedQuestion":"What fire-safety precautions should tour guides take when checking tourists into a hotel so they can evacuate quickly if a fire breaks out?","translatedAnswer":"(1) Learn the locations of the hotel floor’s fire doors, emergency exits, and stairways, as well as the safe evacuation routes, and explain them to the tourists.\n(2) Memorize the fire-service number (119) and the room numbers of the tour leader and tourists."},{"number":6,"translatedQuestion":"A fire breaks out in the hotel, two floors below the rooms occupied by the tour group. How should the tour guide direct the tourists to protect themselves?","translatedAnswer":"Do not let the tourists use the elevator or jump from the building in an attempt to escape. The guide should stay calm, assess the fire, and direct everyone to protect themselves:\n(1) If clothing catches fire, roll on the ground or smother the flames with heavy clothing.\n(2) If it is necessary to pass through thick smoke, cover the body with wet clothing, cover the nose and mouth, and crawl close to the floor along a wall.\n(3) If fire blocks the door and escape is impossible, block gaps around the door with wet clothing or bedding, pour water on them to cool the area, and wait for rescue.\n(4) Wave brightly colored clothing to attract rescuers’ attention."},{"number":7,"translatedQuestion":"How should tour guides respond if a tour group suffers a food-poisoning incident?","translatedAnswer":"(1) Try to induce vomiting in those who have been poisoned and have them drink plenty of water to help flush out the toxins.\n(2) Get the patients to a hospital as quickly as possible and ask the doctor to issue a medical certificate.\n(3) Report the incident to the local receiving travel agency and seek to hold the restaurant responsible."},{"number":8,"translatedQuestion":"The tour group asks at short notice to change a prearranged Western meal to a Chinese meal. How should the local guide handle the request?","translatedAnswer":"(1) If the request is made at least three hours before the meal, the local guide should do their best to contact the restaurant and handle the change in accordance with the relevant rules.\n(2) If the request is made close to mealtime, it will generally not be accepted, but the guide should explain the reason.\n(3) If the tourists insist on changing the meal, the guide may suggest that they order dishes themselves and pay the cost.\n(4) Requests for extra dishes or drinks should be accommodated, with the tourists paying the additional cost."},{"number":9,"translatedQuestion":"When checking a tour group into a hotel, the local guide discovers that some of the reserved standard twin rooms have been replaced with triple rooms. If you were the local guide, what would you do?","translatedAnswer":"(1) Although the triple rooms have the same number of beds, they do not meet the standard for twin rooms specified in the travel agreement. The local receiving travel agency should arrange replacements.\n(2) The local guide should negotiate with the hotel to restore the twin rooms. If this hotel cannot do so, consider a nearby hotel.\n(3) If it is genuinely impossible to make the change, explain the situation to the tourists, ask for their understanding, and compensate them for the difference in price.\n(4) Persuade the tourists to accept the rooms and provide compensation afterward, both material (such as drinks, extra dishes, or souvenirs) and in service (by providing better-quality service)."},{"number":10,"translatedQuestion":"A foreign visitor asks the tour guide to buy an item for them and ship it back to their home country. How should the guide handle this request?","translatedAnswer":"(1) In general, politely decline.\n(2) If it is not possible to decline, seek approval from a supervisor.\n(3) If the request is accepted, handle it carefully: collect enough money from the visitor to cover the purchase and shipping, buy and ship the item, and afterward send the visitor the receipt, shipping form, and shipping-fee receipt. The travel agency should retain copies for inspection."},{"number":11,"translatedQuestion":"How should tour guides help prevent tourists from becoming ill?","translatedAnswer":"(1) Carefully assess the group and plan the itinerary with regard to the age and physical condition of most participants, allowing for an appropriate balance between activity and rest.\n(2) Remind tourists to observe food and drinking-water hygiene and not to drink raw or unclean water.\n(3) Keep up with the weather forecast and remind tourists to add or remove clothing or bring rain gear as conditions change. In dry seasons, remind them to drink more water and eat more fruit."},{"number":12,"translatedQuestion":"A tour group that was due to arrive this morning has, for some reason, been delayed until before dinner tomorrow, shortening its itinerary by a day and a half. What should the local guide do?","translatedAnswer":"(1) First, notify the relevant service providers to cancel today’s accommodation and meals.\n(2) Book accommodation, meals, and transportation for tomorrow.\n(3) Redraw the group’s local itinerary, preserving as many of the originally planned principal sightseeing activities as possible.\n(4) If time is too limited, give priority to the attractions that are most representative and distinctive of the area."},{"number":13,"translatedQuestion":"Because tickets are scarce, the travel agency has to put the tour group on an extra flight that will leave the area earlier than planned. What should the local guide do to gain the tourists’ understanding?","translatedAnswer":"(1) First seek the cooperation of the national guide and tour leader and reach agreement with them.\n(2) Work with influential members of the group.\n(3) Explain the situation honestly to the tourists and apologize sincerely, seeking their understanding.\n(4) Offer appropriate material compensation.\n(5) Use the limited time available to take the tourists to attractions on the itinerary."},{"number":14,"translatedQuestion":"A road closure forces the tour group to cancel a day’s trip to a particular destination, leaving the tourists very disappointed. How should the local guide handle the situation?","translatedAnswer":"(1) Arrange an alternative activity.\n(2) Rekindle the tourists’ interest with an engaging introduction, novel content, and the best possible arrangements.\n(3) Ask the national guide to report the situation to the tour operator that organized the group and obtain its decision."},{"number":15,"translatedQuestion":"When you arrive at the station, the tour group you are to meet has already been waiting outside for a long time. The tourists are very unhappy, and the tour leader accuses you of failing to meet the group. As the local guide, what should you do?","translatedAnswer":"(1) Apologize promptly to the tour leader, national guide, and tourists.\n(2) Explain the situation truthfully and show that you are committed to doing a good job as their guide.\n(3) Take steps to make up for the problem, carry out the reception plan successfully, and earn the tourists’ understanding.\n(4) If necessary, offer the tourists reasonable material compensation."},{"number":16,"translatedQuestion":"A tourist in the group says they do not want to share a twin room with anyone else and asks for a room alone. How would you handle the request appropriately?","translatedAnswer":"(1) If the request is due to a conflict with the other occupant or differences in living habits, ask the tour leader to mediate or arrange a change within the group.\n(2) If mediation or reassignment does not work and the hotel has a vacant room, the request may be granted. However, explain in advance that the tourist must pay the room charge themselves; generally, the person who requests a single room pays for it."},{"number":17,"translatedQuestion":"If a tour group misses its flight, how should the tour guide handle the incident?","translatedAnswer":"(1) Immediately report the matter to the travel agency’s management and the relevant departments and request assistance.\n(2) Contact the airport as soon as possible and arrange for the tourists to leave the current location promptly.\n(3) Reassure the tourists and arrange accommodation, meals, sightseeing, and other necessary matters.\n(4) Notify the next stop promptly and make corresponding adjustments to the itinerary.\n(5) Apologize to the tourists.\n(6) Write an incident report, investigate the cause and determine responsibility. The responsible party should bear the financial losses."},{"number":18,"translatedQuestion":"While sightseeing in Guangzhou, a Chinese American in the tour group accidentally loses their documents. They cannot be found despite an extensive search. As the local guide, how should you handle the matter?","translatedAnswer":"Help the visitor replace their passport and visa; the visitor must pay the costs of obtaining the documents. The procedure is:\n(1) The travel agency first issues a certificate of loss, and the visitor prepares photographs.\n(2) The visitor takes the certificate to the Foreigners’ Entry-Exit Administration Office of the Guangzhou Public Security Bureau to report the loss and obtain a certificate.\n(3) The visitor takes the police certificate to the U.S. Consulate General in Guangzhou and applies for a replacement U.S. passport.\n(4) After receiving the new passport, the visitor returns to the Guangzhou Public Security Bureau to replace the Chinese visa."},{"number":19,"translatedQuestion":"While sightseeing in Guangzhou, a Chinese expatriate from the United States in the tour group accidentally loses their documents, which cannot be found despite an extensive search. As the local guide, how should you help them replace their passport and visa?","translatedAnswer":"(1) Ask the visitor to prepare photographs.\n(2) Ask the local receiving travel agency to issue a certificate of loss.\n(3) The visitor takes the certificate of loss to the Guangdong Provincial Public Security Department to report the loss and apply for a replacement Chinese passport.\n(4) With the new passport, the visitor goes to the U.S. Consulate General in Guangzhou to obtain a visa for entry into the United States."},{"number":20,"translatedQuestion":"A domestic tourist in the group accidentally loses their ID card during the trip, and the group must travel by air. How should the tour guide handle the matter?","translatedAnswer":"(1) The local travel agency issues a certificate of loss.\n(2) The tourist goes in person to the local public security bureau with the certificate to report the loss and request an identity certificate.\n(3) If an identity document cannot be issued, arrange for the tourist to use another means of transportation. The tourist initially pays the transportation cost; after the trip, settle it reasonably in accordance with the agreement or relevant regulations."},{"number":21,"translatedQuestion":"A foreign visitor’s baggage is lost while they are travelling to China, and they discover the loss only after arriving at a Chinese airport. How should the local guide assist?","translatedAnswer":"(1) Take the visitor to the airport’s lost-property office to report the missing baggage and complete the claim and recovery procedures.\n(2) During local sightseeing, the local guide should call from time to time to ask about the search for the baggage.\n(3) If the baggage cannot be found promptly, help the visitor purchase essential daily necessities.\n(4) If the baggage is confirmed lost, help the visitor claim compensation from the relevant airline."},{"number":22,"translatedQuestion":"Before a tour group leaves an area, its members express strong dissatisfaction with the food there. What remedial steps should the guide take?","translatedAnswer":"(1) Listen carefully to the tourists’ comments and make a record.\n(2) Inform the local travel agency and hotel of the situation and suggest improvements. If possible, ask them to apologize to the tourists and take necessary remedial measures.\n(3) Find out about the tourists’ dining habits and inform the travel agency at the next stop so it can prepare special services in advance."},{"number":23,"translatedQuestion":"What care should the national guide provide to tourists in the group who suffer from motion sickness when travelling by car, plane, or boat?","translatedAnswer":"(1) Remind those who suffer from motion sickness not to travel on a full stomach and to take appropriate medication before travelling. They should preferably use their own medication, and be reminded to check its contraindications; many motion-sickness medicines are unsuitable for people with glaucoma.\n(2) Whenever possible, arrange for them to sit in a relatively stable seat.\n(3) On a long journey, if a tourist becomes motion-sick in a plane, vehicle, or boat, the guide may ask a crew member to help care for them."},{"number":24,"translatedQuestion":"A tourist in the group collapses from heatstroke. What measures should the tour guide take?","translatedAnswer":"(1) Move the patient to a shady place, have them lie flat, loosen their collar, and loosen their belt.\n(2) If possible, give them a drink containing salt or necessary heatstroke medication. If they have a fever, cool them by wiping their body with cold water or alcohol.\n(3) Once the symptoms ease, have them sit or lie quietly and rest.\n(4) After providing necessary first aid, take anyone suffering severe heatstroke to a hospital immediately."},{"number":25,"translatedQuestion":"When a tour group checks into a hotel, what should the guide do to protect tourists from harm and protect their belongings?","translatedAnswer":"(1) After check-in, advise tourists to put valuables in the hotel safe rather than carrying them around or leaving them in their rooms.\n(2) Remind tourists to lock their doors when entering or leaving their rooms and not to give their room number to strangers. They should not let strangers or people claiming to be hotel repair workers enter their rooms freely, and should be especially cautious about opening the door at night. They should not exchange foreign currency with private individuals."},{"number":26,"translatedQuestion":"The local guide arrives at the airport on time as scheduled to meet a tour group, but the group does not arrive. What should the guide do?","translatedAnswer":"(1) Immediately contact the relevant department of the guide’s travel agency to find out the reason.\n(2) If the group’s arrival will be delayed only briefly, remain at the meeting point and continue waiting.\n(3) If the delay will be lengthy, follow the agency’s arrangements and make new plans for receiving the group."},{"number":27,"translatedQuestion":"A local guide meets a group at the airport as scheduled, but only discovers on arrival at the hotel that it is not the group they were supposed to meet. What should the guide do?","translatedAnswer":"(1) Find out what caused the mistake.\n(2) Determine where the guide’s assigned group is. If a guide from another travel agency picked it up, contact them as soon as possible and exchange the groups immediately.\n(3) If a guide from the same travel agency picked up the assigned group, the local guide generally need not switch groups, but the national guide must be changed.\n(4) Explain the situation truthfully to the tourists and apologize sincerely."},{"number":28,"translatedQuestion":"After completing the itinerary in China under the group’s original reception plan, several foreign tourists ask to stay and continue sightseeing. How should the national guide handle the request?","translatedAnswer":"(1) After consulting the travel agency, provide necessary assistance if approved. Accompany the tourists to the local public security bureau with their own passports, a letter from the travel agency, and the group’s collective visa to apply for separate visas and visa extensions. Help them rebook transportation tickets and accommodation. The tourists must pay the costs.\n(2) If they want the travel agency to continue providing services such as guiding, they must sign a separate contract."},{"number":29,"translatedQuestion":"A tour bus accidentally overturns, leaving three people seriously injured and many others slightly injured. If you were the group’s tour guide, what measures would you take?","translatedAnswer":"(1) Organize rescue efforts immediately.\n(2) Protect the scene and report the incident to the police immediately.\n(3) Report the matter promptly to the travel agency.\n(4) Reassure the tourists in the group.\n(5) Write a report on the incident."},{"number":30,"translatedQuestion":"What should tour guides do to help prevent traffic accidents during a tour?","translatedAnswer":"(1) Before departure, remind the driver to inspect the vehicle and promptly address any hazards or replace the vehicle.\n(2) Allow sufficient time in the itinerary; do not urge the driver to speed or violate traffic rules to stay on schedule.\n(3) When weather, traffic, or road conditions are poor, remind the driver to take care while driving.\n(4) Do not allow anyone who is not the assigned driver to drive the vehicle.\n(5) Remind the driver not to drink alcohol."},{"number":31,"translatedQuestion":"How should a tour guide help prevent a tour group from suffering food poisoning?","translatedAnswer":"(1) Arrange for the group to eat only at designated restaurants.\n(2) Remind tourists not to buy food from street stalls.\n(3) If food is found to be unclean or spoiled during a meal, immediately ask for it to be replaced."},{"number":32,"translatedQuestion":"A tourist from Taiwan discovers that their travel document is missing. How should the local guide handle the situation?","translatedAnswer":"The local receiving travel agency should issue a certificate. The tourist should take the certificate, documents and other materials that establish their identity, and photographs to the exit-entry administration office of the municipal public security bureau where the document was lost to report the loss. After verifying the information, the bureau will issue a single-use entry-exit permit."},{"number":33,"translatedQuestion":"A tourist cannot find their baggage at the airport carousel. What should the national guide do?","translatedAnswer":"(1) Take the tourist to the lost-property office to report the missing baggage and complete the claim and recovery procedures. The tourist should present their air ticket and baggage tag and fill out a missing-baggage form.\n(2) Report the matter to the travel agency’s management immediately and ask it to arrange for the relevant departments and staff to contact the airport, the travel agency at the previous stop, the airline, and other parties to search actively."},{"number":34,"translatedQuestion":"A married couple in the tour group asks to eat in their hotel room. How should the tour guide handle the request?","translatedAnswer":"(1) Explain the situation patiently and ask the tour leader to encourage them to eat with the group.\n(2) If they still insist on eating in their room, tell them to contact the restaurant directly to arrange room service. They must pay for the meal, and the comprehensive service fee will not be refunded."},{"number":35,"translatedQuestion":"A tourist feels unwell and wants to stay at the hotel alone to rest instead of joining the group’s activities. What should the guide do?","translatedAnswer":"(1) Let the tourist decide for themselves and do not pressure them to join the group.\n(2) Notify the restaurant to arrange lunch and, if necessary, provide room service.\n(3) Tell the tourist how to contact the hotel clinic.\n(4) Ask the room attendant to look after the tourist.\n(5) After returning from the excursion, check on the tourist to show concern."},{"number":36,"translatedQuestion":"A foreign tourist has an urgent matter and asks to leave the tour early and return home. How should the tour guide handle the request?","translatedAnswer":"(1) Report the tourist’s request to the travel agency immediately. After the receiving agency consults the tour operator that organized the group, the request may be granted. Under the travel agreement, the cost of comprehensive services not used may be refunded in part or not at all.\n(2) The guide should also help the tourist rebook transportation, obtain a separate visa, and complete other departure procedures. The tourist must pay the costs."},{"number":37,"translatedQuestion":"While the group is sightseeing, a photography enthusiast asks to walk separately from the group. You are the local guide. How would you make suitable arrangements?","translatedAnswer":"If circumstances permit—for example, if the attraction is not crowded and order is not disrupted—the request may be accommodated. Remind the tourist of the meeting time and place and the tour vehicle’s number. If necessary, leave the tourist a note stating the meeting time and place, vehicle number, and the hotel’s name and telephone number."},{"number":38,"translatedQuestion":"A tourist asks to return or exchange an item after buying it in a shop. Should the tour guide help?","translatedAnswer":"The tour guide should actively assist and, if necessary, accompany the tourist to the shop."},{"number":39,"translatedQuestion":"A train cannot stop wherever passengers wish. If a tourist becomes seriously ill while the group is travelling by train, what should the national guide do?","translatedAnswer":"Immediately ask the train crew to find a doctor on board to treat the tourist, and notify the emergency center and travel agency at the next station so they can prepare to meet the train and provide emergency care."},{"number":40,"translatedQuestion":"A tourist asks the guide to organize a visit to a nearby beach for swimming. What should the guide do?","translatedAnswer":"(1) Persuade the tourists not to swim in natural waters without safety measures.\n(2) If necessary, arrange for them to swim at a pool instead.\n(3) The guide must not leave the rest of the group unattended to accompany only a few people swimming.\n(4) Even at a swimming pool, remind swimmers to take care for their safety and avoid drowning."}]},"knowledge":{"category":"knowledge","items":[{"number":1,"translatedQuestion":"Please tell us about the origin of the name “Guangzhou.”","translatedAnswer":"During the Eastern Wu period of the Three Kingdoms, the area north of Hepu was designated Guangzhou and administered the commanderies of Nanhai, Cangwu, Yulin, and others. The name “Guangzhou” dates from this designation."},{"number":2,"translatedQuestion":"Please describe the climate of Guangdong.","translatedAnswer":"Guangdong lies in the subtropical zone and has a mild climate with abundant rainfall. Its average annual temperature is about 22.3°C, and its average annual precipitation is 1,700 millimeters."},{"number":3,"translatedQuestion":"Please talk about the history of Guangdong during the Pre-Qin period.","translatedAnswer":"During the Pre-Qin period, this area was inhabited by the Nanyue people, one of the Baiyue peoples. In 219 BCE, Qin Shi Huang unified Lingnan and established the three commanderies of Guilin, Xiang, and Nanhai. Present-day Guangzhou was the seat of Nanhai Commandery."},{"number":4,"translatedQuestion":"What do you know about the Kingdom of Nanyue?","translatedAnswer":"In the early Western Han period, Zhao Tuo, a former Qin general, established the first local regime in Lingnan, the Kingdom of Nanyue, in what is now Guangdong. It promoted integration between the Han and Yue peoples and accelerated social, economic, and cultural development in Lingnan."},{"number":5,"translatedQuestion":"What are the four famous gardens of Guangdong Province?","translatedAnswer":"Qinghui Garden, Keyuan Garden, Yuyin Shanfang Garden, and Liang Garden are the four most famous gardens in Guangdong and are representative of Lingnan gardens."},{"number":6,"translatedQuestion":"What are China’s four famous embroidery traditions?","translatedAnswer":"Guangdong embroidery, together with Suzhou embroidery, Hunan embroidery, and Sichuan embroidery, is known as one of China’s “Four Great Embroideries.”"},{"number":7,"translatedQuestion":"Can you introduce the three major regional communities in Guangdong?","translatedAnswer":"They are the Guangfu, Chaoshan, and Hakka communities, corresponding respectively to Guangfu, Chaoshan, and Hakka culture and embodying the essence of Lingnan culture. The Guangfu community formed earliest among the three and is concentrated mainly in the Pearl River Delta and in some counties and cities in central and western Guangdong."},{"number":8,"translatedQuestion":"What is the most apparent feature that distinguishes the three communities?","translatedAnswer":"Their dialects: “Baihua,” based on the Guangzhou dialect; Hakka, based on the Meixian dialect; and Chaoshan, based on the Shantou dialect."},{"number":9,"translatedQuestion":"Cantonese cuisine is one of China’s four major regional cuisines. Can you introduce the three main styles of cuisine in Guangdong?","translatedAnswer":"As the saying goes, “The best food is in Guangdong.” Cantonese cuisine mainly comprises Guangzhou, Chaozhou, and Dongjiang styles. Guangzhou cuisine is representative of Cantonese cuisine; its dishes are light, fresh, tender, smooth, and crisp, with great attention to color, aroma, flavor, and presentation. Chaozhou cuisine is known for seafood, especially its soups. Dongjiang cuisine, also known as Hakka cuisine, features many meat dishes, uses generous amounts of oil, and tends to be somewhat salty; clay-pot dishes are a specialty."},{"number":10,"translatedQuestion":"Architecture in Guangdong, as a vehicle of Lingnan culture, has a distinctive style. Can you name some of the province’s unique vernacular buildings?","translatedAnswer":"Examples include distinctive Lingnan residences such as Xiguan mansions, arcaded buildings, and Hakka walled compounds; ceremonial buildings such as the Nanhai God Temple, Foshan Ancestral Temple, Chen Clan Ancestral Hall, and Panyu Academy; the Kaiping Diaolou, a World Heritage property; and defensive structures such as Xinhui Yamen Fort and Guangzhou’s Zhenhai Tower. All display the characteristics of Lingnan architecture."},{"number":11,"translatedQuestion":"Please explain the origin of the name of Kaiyuan Temple in Chaozhou, Guangdong.","translatedAnswer":"According to historical records, Kaiyuan Temple was originally called Lifeng Temple and was founded around the Sui-Tang transition. In the 26th year of the Kaiyuan era (738 CE), Emperor Xuanzong of Tang ordered each of the country’s 81 prefectures and counties to select a major temple and name it after his era name, Kaiyuan, in celebration of his birthday. Chaozhou accordingly renamed Lifeng Temple Kaiyuan Temple."},{"number":12,"translatedQuestion":"What can we learn from the jade seals excavated from the tomb of the King of Nanyue?","translatedAnswer":"Of the nine jade seals, three were found on the occupant’s body in the main coffin chamber. They were inscribed “Zhao Mo,” “Crown Prince,” and “Seal of the Emperor,” respectively, and provide physical evidence that Zhao Mo usurped the title of emperor."},{"number":13,"translatedQuestion":"Please introduce the Zidong pleasure boat in Baomo Garden, Panyu, Guangdong.","translatedAnswer":"The Zidong pleasure boat is characteristic of the Pearl River Delta. It is lavishly decorated, with dining tables at the bow and a kitchen at the stern. Such boats were most popular during the Ming and Qing dynasties. Originally used as pleasure craft by wealthy families, they later developed into teahouses on the water."},{"number":14,"translatedQuestion":"What is the meaning of the stone relief titled “Noble Rank, Official Emoluments, and Enfeoffment as a Marquis” on the wall behind the stone drum to the right of the entrance to the Chen Clan Ancestral Hall in Guangzhou?","translatedAnswer":"The relief is an allegorical image showing a bird, a deer, a bee, and a monkey. Their Chinese names are homophones for “noble rank,” “official emoluments,” “enfeoffment,” and “marquis,” respectively, together conveying the wish that members of the Chen family would receive noble titles and lucrative official appointments."},{"number":15,"translatedQuestion":"Where do Guangdong’s ethnic minorities mainly live, and can you tell us about their customs?","translatedAnswer":"Guangdong’s ethnic minorities are concentrated mainly in areas such as Liannan and Ruyuan. Their distinctive customs include the Yao people’s Getang singing gatherings and cloth-bag wooden-lion tradition; the Zhuang people’s April Eighth festival and Ox King’s Birthday; and the She people’s Second Day of the Second Month and Third Day of the Third Month festivals."},{"number":16,"translatedQuestion":"What kind of regime was the Kingdom of Nanyue? How many generations of rulers did it have, how long did it last, and in what year and under which dynasty did it come under central rule?","translatedAnswer":"The Kingdom of Nanyue was a local regime in southern China during the Western Han period. It had five successive generations of rulers and lasted 93 years. In the sixth year of Emperor Wu’s Yuanding era (111 BCE), it came under the Western Han."},{"number":17,"translatedQuestion":"What are some of the well-known forest parks in Guangdong Province?","translatedAnswer":"They include Liuxi River Forest Park, Xiqiao Mountain Forest Park, Nanling National Forest Park, Nankunshan Forest Park, and Shimen Forest Bathing Area, among other rich forest-tourism resources…"},{"number":18,"translatedQuestion":"Please tell us what you know about Guangdong carving and its well-known traditional crafts.","translatedAnswer":"Guangdong carving, represented by ivory, jade, and wood carving, is delicate and highly accomplished, with an exceptionally high artistic standard. The province’s distinctive crafts include Duan inkstones from Zhaoqing, paper-cutting and lion-head making in Foshan, palm-leaf crafts in Xinhui, and lacquerware in Yangjiang. These crafts all have long histories and a high level of artistry."},{"number":19,"translatedQuestion":"Why is the architectural decorative art of the Chen Clan Ancestral Hall said to have a strong local Guangdong character?","translatedAnswer":"From top to bottom and inside and out, its columns, eaves, ridges, steps, doors, windows, and other features are adorned with woodcarvings, brick and stone carvings, ceramic and gray-plaster sculptures, ironwork, and other crafts. These works are not only decorative; they also draw on historical and mythological stories, while motifs composed of flowers, birds, insects, fish, and other creatures carry symbolic meanings. For example, peonies symbolize wealth and prosperity, paired lotus flowers symbolize marital devotion, and lions symbolize power and dignity."},{"number":20,"translatedQuestion":"Why is the Ancestral Temple in Foshan known as the “Palace of Oriental Folk Art”?","translatedAnswer":"Its ridges, eaves, walls, and interior are decorated with ceramic and gray-plaster sculptures, brick carvings, woodcarvings, and other works depicting traditional Chinese folk stories and myths. These decorations are finely crafted, with lifelike figures, and are precious examples of ancient Chinese art. For this reason, the Ancestral Temple is known as the “Palace of Oriental Folk Art.”"},{"number":21,"translatedQuestion":"Why is Huineng, together with Confucius and Laozi, called one of the “Three Great Sages of the East” and regarded by European scholars as one of the world’s ten great thinkers?","translatedAnswer":"Huineng’s doctrine of sudden enlightenment had a profound impact on Buddhism, making Chan the mainstream school of Chinese Buddhism, and also greatly influenced Chinese philosophical thought. For this reason, he is regarded alongside Confucius and Laozi as one of the “Three Great Sages of the East”; European scholars have also called him one of the world’s ten great thinkers."},{"number":22,"translatedQuestion":"Can you list the types of carving and other crafted works used to decorate the Chen Clan Ancestral Hall?","translatedAnswer":"Craftworks found throughout the hall include stone carvings, brick carvings, gray-plaster sculptures, ceramic sculptures, woodcarvings, and iron-cast components."},{"number":23,"translatedQuestion":"What does “Xiguan mansion” mean in Guangzhou?","translatedAnswer":"In the lanes around Shangxiajiu Road, there are residences with distinctive forms and structures. Formerly homes of wealthy merchants, they are known as Xiguan mansions. These houses are large and sturdy, and both their interiors and exteriors are decorated with woodcarvings, stone carvings, ceramic sculptures, and gray-plaster sculptures. They were among Guangzhou’s most representative residential buildings of their time."},{"number":24,"translatedQuestion":"What are the four most famous temples in Guangdong Province?","translatedAnswer":"They are Kaiyuan Temple in Chaozhou, Nanhua Temple in Shaoguan, Qingyun Temple in Zhaoqing, and Guangxiao Temple in Guangzhou."},{"number":25,"translatedQuestion":"Five of the ten generals of the Chinese People’s Liberation Army graduated from the Whampoa Military Academy. Can you name them?","translatedAnswer":"They are Ye Jianying, Nie Rongzhen, Chen Yi, Xu Xiangqian, and Lin Biao."},{"number":26,"translatedQuestion":"Why are the stone drums on either side of the main entrance regarded as a symbol of the Chen family’s status?","translatedAnswer":"In feudal China, a family could place a pair of large drums in front of its residence only if one of its members had attained at least the jinshi degree in the imperial examinations. In the 18th year of the Guangxu reign (1893), the year before the Chen Clan Ancestral Hall was completed, Chen Botao of the Chen family attained the rank of tanhua, the third-place laureate in the highest imperial examination. The stone drums were therefore installed in his honor."},{"number":27,"translatedQuestion":"What are “cliff inscriptions”?","translatedAnswer":"“Cliff inscriptions” are images and writing carved into relatively flat surfaces of natural rock, including records of achievements and events. They are usually created jointly by literati calligraphers and stonemasons."},{"number":28,"translatedQuestion":"What are the four most famous mountains in the Lingnan region?","translatedAnswer":"Danxia Mountain, Luofu Mountain, Xiqiao Mountain, and Dinghu Mountain are the four most famous mountains in the Lingnan region."},{"number":29,"translatedQuestion":"In July 2026, the Publicity Department of the CPC Guangdong Provincial Committee, the Guangdong Provincial Department of Culture and Tourism, and the Guangdong Provincial Department of Education jointly issued guidelines for premium study-tour themes and routes in Guangdong under two categories: humanities and science and technology. What specific themes are included in the science and technology category?","translatedAnswer":"The science and technology category of the Guangdong premium study-tour themes comprises four themes: AI-powered manufacturing, electronic information, biomedicine, and digital cultural and creative industries."},{"number":30,"translatedQuestion":"In July 2026, the Ministry of Culture and Tourism issued the 15th Five-Year Plan for Building China into a Tourism Power. What five major missions does it set out for accelerating this effort over the next five years?","translatedAnswer":"The five missions are to enable tourism to better serve people’s aspirations for a better life; promote economic development; build a spiritual home; showcase China’s image; and foster mutual learning among civilizations."},{"number":31,"translatedQuestion":"Where was the main event for 2026 China Tourism Day, held on May 19, and what was this year’s theme?","translatedAnswer":"The main event for 2026 China Tourism Day was held in Guangzhou, Guangdong Province. This year’s theme was “Enjoy Quality Travel and Explore Beautiful Landscapes Together.”"},{"number":32,"translatedQuestion":"On July 25, 2026, at the 48th session of the UNESCO World Heritage Committee in Busan, South Korea, which Chinese nomination was inscribed on the World Heritage List? How many World Heritage properties does China have in total as a result?","translatedAnswer":"At the 48th session of the UNESCO World Heritage Committee in Busan, South Korea, China’s nomination “Jingdezhen Handicraft Porcelain Industry Sites” was inscribed on the World Heritage List, bringing the country’s total to 61 properties."},{"number":33,"translatedQuestion":"On December 11, 2025, which Chinese element was transferred to the Representative List of the Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity? What is the resulting total number of Chinese elements on UNESCO’s intangible cultural heritage lists and register?","translatedAnswer":"Hezhen Yimakan storytelling was transferred to the Representative List of the Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity, bringing the total number of Chinese elements on UNESCO’s intangible cultural heritage lists and register to 45."},{"number":34,"translatedQuestion":"Starting November 5, 2025, which five ports in Guangdong Province did the National Immigration Administration add to the 240-hour visa-free transit policy?","translatedAnswer":"The five ports are Guangzhou Pazhou Passenger Port, Hengqin Port, Hong Kong-Zhuhai-Macao Bridge Port, Zhongshan Port, and the West Kowloon Station port of the Guangzhou–Shenzhen–Hong Kong Express Rail Link."},{"number":35,"translatedQuestion":"From March to April 2025, the American online influencer known as “Hyperthyroidism Bro” explored and documented authentic Chinese life and scenery from a more independent, immersive, personal perspective, becoming an enthusiastic grassroots ambassador for China’s image. Which cities did he visit on his trip to China?","translatedAnswer":"Shanghai, Beijing, Dengfeng in Henan (including the Shaolin Temple), Chengdu, Chongqing, Hong Kong, Shenzhen, and Changsha."},{"number":36,"translatedQuestion":"In 2025, “China shopping” became a global sensation. What were the “old three” and “new three” on the shopping lists of foreign visitors coming to China?","translatedAnswer":"The “old three” are silk, tea, and porcelain; the “new three” are small household appliances, drones, and trendy cultural and creative products."},{"number":37,"translatedQuestion":"Starting on May 10, 2025, which league became a phenomenal cultural-tourism sensation, propelled by online catchphrases such as “Competition first, friendship fourteenth” and “the Thirteen Taibao,” as well as highly entertaining matches?","translatedAnswer":"The Jiangsu City Football League, commonly known as the “Su Super League.”"},{"number":38,"translatedQuestion":"During China Brand Day in May 2026, which Guangdong brands were included among the first 30 recommended “Travel with Brands” experience destinations?","translatedAnswer":"DJI and Fengyi Smart Logistics, both based in Shenzhen."}]},"zh-en":{"category":"zh-en","items":[{"number":1,"translatedQuestion":"孙中山先生是中国伟大的资产阶级民主革命的先行者，1866年11月12日出生于广东香山县（即今中山市）翠亨村。他12岁时随兄去美国夏威夷教会学校读书，后回香港西医学堂学医，学成后在广州和澳门行医。","translatedAnswer":"Dr. Sun Yat-sen, a great pioneer of China\u0027s bourgeois-democratic revolution, was born on November 12, 1866, in Cuiheng Village, Xiangshan County (present-day Zhongshan City), Guangdong. At the age of 12, he accompanied his elder brother to Hawaii in the United States to study at a missionary school. He later returned to Hong Kong to study medicine at a Western medical college, and after completing his studies practiced medicine in Guangzhou and Macao."},{"number":2,"translatedQuestion":"孙中山早期曾对清政府存有幻想，曾向清朝统治者提出革新政治的主张；但帝国主义列强对中国的侵略和清政府的腐败无能使他认识到，要振兴中华就必须推翻满清王朝，建立民国。","translatedAnswer":"In his early years, Sun Yat-sen still held hopes for the Qing government and put forward proposals for political reform to the Qing rulers. However, the imperialist powers\u0027 aggression against China and the Qing government\u0027s corruption and incompetence made him realize that China could be revitalized only by overthrowing the Manchu Qing dynasty and establishing a republic."},{"number":3,"translatedQuestion":"1905年，孙中山在日本成立了中国第一个革命政党同盟会（即国民党的前身），致力于推翻清朝的革命斗争，并最终于1911年发动的武昌起义中取得成功，建立了中华民国，孙中山被推选为临时大总统。","translatedAnswer":"In 1905, Sun Yat-sen founded the Tongmenghui in Japan, China\u0027s first revolutionary political party and the forerunner of the Kuomintang. It pursued the revolutionary struggle to overthrow the Qing dynasty and ultimately succeeded in the Wuchang Uprising of 1911. The Republic of China was established, and Sun Yat-sen was elected provisional president."},{"number":4,"translatedQuestion":"孙中山为中国的民主革命奋斗终生，他所领导的辛亥革命推翻了在中国存在了几千年的封建帝制；广州人民为了纪念他的功绩，在当年南方革命政府总统府所在地建造了这座中山纪念堂。","translatedAnswer":"Sun Yat-sen devoted his entire life to China\u0027s democratic revolution. The 1911 Revolution he led overthrew the feudal imperial system that had existed in China for thousands of years. To commemorate his achievements, the people of Guangzhou built this Sun Yat-sen Memorial Hall on the site of the former presidential headquarters of the Southern Revolutionary Government."},{"number":5,"translatedQuestion":"中山纪念堂于1931年10月建成，其主体建筑高58米，建筑面积1.2万平方米。它是一座八角形的宫殿式钢筋混凝土建筑；在外形上具有中国传统建筑艺术风格，在结构上则采用了当时最新的建筑技术。","translatedAnswer":"The Sun Yat-sen Memorial Hall was completed in October 1931. Its main building is 58 meters high and has a floor area of 12,000 square meters. It is an octagonal, palace-style reinforced-concrete building: its exterior reflects the style of traditional Chinese architecture, while its structure employed the most advanced building technology of the time."},{"number":6,"translatedQuestion":"中山纪念堂是一个能容纳3238名观众的大会堂；会堂内看不到一根支撑柱子，整个大屋顶由4个大跨度的大型钢桁架支撑，8根柱子都巧妙地隐藏在墙内。观众坐在大堂内任何一个座位都不会被柱子遮档视线；而且，堂内没有回音，音响效果极好。","translatedAnswer":"The Sun Yat-sen Memorial Hall is an auditorium that can seat 3,238 people. No supporting columns are visible inside: the enormous roof is carried by four large-span steel trusses, and all eight columns are cleverly concealed in the walls. From every seat in the auditorium, spectators have an unobstructed view. The hall is also free of echoes and has excellent acoustics."},{"number":7,"translatedQuestion":"开平碉楼是集防卫、居住和中西建筑艺术于一体的建筑, 是国务院公布的国家重点文物保护单位。开平碉楼在鼎盛时期达3300多座，目前登记在册的有1833座。","translatedAnswer":"Kaiping diaolou are buildings that combine defensive and residential functions with Chinese and Western architectural styles. They are listed by the State Council as major sites of cultural heritage under state protection. At their peak, there were more than 3,300 diaolou in Kaiping; 1,833 are currently registered."},{"number":8,"translatedQuestion":"你可能想知道为什么这些海外华侨回国要把房子建成碉楼的结构，这其实很明显。首先，那个时代的公共秩序很糟糕，对这些归国华侨家庭而言，他们自然就成为抢劫的目标了，土匪令他们非常头疼；第二，开平地区地势低矮，洪水经常光顾。所以，这些坚固高大的建筑不仅能够防御土匪，也成为洪灾的避难所了。","translatedAnswer":"You may wonder why these overseas Chinese built their homes as watchtowers when they returned to China. The reasons are quite clear. First, public order was very poor at the time, and these returning families naturally became targets for robbery; bandits were a serious problem for them. Second, Kaiping is low-lying and was frequently struck by floods. These sturdy, tall buildings could therefore serve both as protection from bandits and as refuges during floods."},{"number":9,"translatedQuestion":"其中最精美的碉楼是铭石楼，该楼建于1925年，是钢筋混凝土结构的居楼。楼高5层，顶部正中有一中西合璧的六角形瞭望塔，第五层四角均建有角堡。铭石楼楼身宽大，外形壮观，有厚重的铁门和坚固的铁窗，内部陈设豪华，生活设施齐全。","translatedAnswer":"The most exquisite of these watchtowers is Mingshi Lou. Built in 1925 as a reinforced-concrete residence, it has five stories. At the center of its roof is a hexagonal lookout tower blending Chinese and Western styles, and each corner of the fifth story has a turret. Mingshi Lou is broad and imposing, with heavy iron doors and sturdy iron windows. Its interior is luxuriously furnished and fully equipped for daily life."},{"number":10,"translatedQuestion":"瑞石楼是开平最高、最豪华的碉楼，楼高25米共9层，楼内设施布局典雅，具有中国传统格调，是开平碉楼第一楼。该楼属钢筋混凝土结构。","translatedAnswer":"Ruishi Lou is the tallest and most luxurious watchtower in Kaiping. It stands 25 meters high and has nine stories. Its facilities are arranged with elegance and its interior has a traditional Chinese character, making it the foremost of Kaiping\u0027s diaolou. The building is made of reinforced concrete."},{"number":11,"translatedQuestion":"方氏灯楼是开平最具代表性的碉楼，建于1920年，楼内的警用发电机，探照灯，枪械一应俱全，选地视野开阔，是典型的防守更楼，属钢筋混凝土结构。","translatedAnswer":"The Fang Family Watchtower is one of the most representative diaolou in Kaiping. Built in 1920, it was fully equipped with a generator for the police, searchlights, and firearms. It occupies an open site with a broad view and is a typical defensive watchtower. It is made of reinforced concrete."},{"number":12,"translatedQuestion":"花园的名字“立”源于园主的名字，表达了中国格言“修身立本”的含义，这四个字写在花园的拱门上，意为修炼个人的道德和性格是生活和工作成功的关键。","translatedAnswer":"The garden\u0027s name, “Li” (立), comes from its owner\u0027s name and conveys the meaning of the Chinese saying “cultivate oneself and establish one\u0027s foundation” (修身立本). These four characters are inscribed on an archway in the garden. They mean that cultivating one\u0027s morals and character is the key to success in life and work."},{"number":13,"translatedQuestion":"丹霞山位于韶关市东北部，距市区45公里。2500万年以前，那里原是一片低平的湖泊，后来因地壳运动的作用，地面上升，湖水尽退，形成了高耸的山峰；原来湖底的沉积物受到氧化作用，变成了红色的岩石。","translatedAnswer":"Danxia Mountain lies 45 kilometers northeast of Shaoguan city. Twenty-five million years ago, the area was a broad, shallow lake. Later, movements of the Earth\u0027s crust raised the land and the lake drained away, forming towering peaks. The sediments on the former lakebed were oxidized and turned into red rock."},{"number":14,"translatedQuestion":"丹霞山的总面积为319平方公里，有大小石峰600多座，宛如一方红宝石雕塑园，故又称“中国红石公园”，并于1995年被国务院批准为国家地质自然保护区。","translatedAnswer":"Danxia Mountain covers an area of 319 square kilometers and has more than 600 stone peaks of various sizes. It resembles a sculpture garden carved from rubies, which is why it is also known as “China\u0027s Red Stone Park.” In 1995, the State Council approved it as a national geological nature reserve."},{"number":15,"translatedQuestion":"（在阳元大桥上）大家往右看：那座山就像一个入睡的少女，从右到左是头部、颈部、胸部和腹部，轮廓分明；故此景叫“睡美人”或“玉女拦江”。","translatedAnswer":"(From Yangyuan Bridge) Please look to your right. That mountain resembles a sleeping young woman: from right to left, you can clearly make out her head, neck, chest, and abdomen. This sight is therefore known as “Sleeping Beauty” or “the Jade Maiden Blocking the River.”"},{"number":16,"translatedQuestion":"别传寺建于清朝康熙元年（1662年），清朝时香火极盛，是粤北三大佛教寺院之一。","translatedAnswer":"Biechuan Temple was built in 1662, the first year of the Kangxi reign in the Qing dynasty. It was a thriving place of worship during the Qing dynasty and was one of the three major Buddhist temples in northern Guangdong."},{"number":17,"translatedQuestion":"阴元石的形成已有 10 多万年了。上帝创造了伊甸园中的亚当和夏娃一对夫妻，而大自然则在丹霞山雕琢出这对夫妻石。世界就是这么奇妙！","translatedAnswer":"The Yinyuan Stone took shape more than 100,000 years ago. God created Adam and Eve as a couple in the Garden of Eden, while nature sculpted this pair of rocks at Danxia Mountain. The world is truly full of wonders!"},{"number":18,"translatedQuestion":"在大雄宝殿内有一石壁，状似龙，其颜色会因春夏秋冬四季不同而变化；春而嫩绿，夏则深绿，秋为黄绿，冬变褐黄；故被称作变色龙。","translatedAnswer":"Inside the Mahavira Hall is a stone wall shaped like a dragon. Its color changes with the seasons: tender green in spring, deep green in summer, yellow-green in autumn, and brownish yellow in winter. It is therefore called the Chameleon."},{"number":19,"translatedQuestion":"活人殉葬现象商、周时期曾在中原地区盛行，但至汉朝时已基本不存在。南越王墓中发现众多殉人，说明这种残酷的殉葬制度当时仍被南越国统治阶级所沿用。","translatedAnswer":"The practice of burying living people with the dead was once widespread in the Central Plains during the Shang and Zhou dynasties, but had largely disappeared by the Han dynasty. The many human victims found in the tomb of the King of Nanyue show that the kingdom\u0027s ruling class still practiced this cruel form of burial at that time."},{"number":20,"translatedQuestion":"此处在南越国宫殿中的位置正好与长安汉皇宫中的长乐宫所处的位置相符，它是否就是南越国宫殿中的“长乐宫”？此推断还有待证实。","translatedAnswer":"This spot occupies the same position in the Nanyue palace as Changle Palace did in the Han imperial palace at Chang\u0027an. Could it have been the “Changle Palace” of the Nanyue palace? This hypothesis remains to be confirmed."},{"number":21,"translatedQuestion":"玉衣是汉代特有的丧葬殓服，通常为金镂、银镂或铜镂，而南越王墓出土地的玉衣却是丝镂，为首次发现，也是至今发现的唯一的一件丝镂玉衣。","translatedAnswer":"Jade burial suits were a distinctive funerary garment of the Han dynasty, usually sewn with gold, silver, or copper wire. The jade suit unearthed from the tomb of the King of Nanyue, however, was sewn with silk thread. It was the first of its kind ever discovered and remains the only silk-thread jade burial suit found to date."},{"number":22,"translatedQuestion":"出土的56块玉璧中有47块在主棺室；其中一块直径达33.4厘米，是我国考古出土玉璧中形体最大的一块玉璧。","translatedAnswer":"Of the 56 jade bi discs unearthed, 47 were found in the main coffin chamber. One of them measures 33.4 centimeters in diameter, making it the largest jade bi disc ever excavated in China."},{"number":23,"translatedQuestion":"出土的铜虎节是一件国内仅存的错金虎节。节是外交和军事上的信符，可以用来征调战车和士兵。","translatedAnswer":"The bronze tiger tally unearthed here is the only surviving tiger tally in China inlaid with gold. A tally was an official token used in diplomacy and military affairs, and could be used to call up chariots and soldiers."},{"number":24,"translatedQuestion":"它们制作工艺精湛，极具地方特色，不但反映出南越国当时的铸造技术水平，而且是广州建城历史的重要物证。","translatedAnswer":"These objects are finely crafted and strongly reflect local characteristics. They not only demonstrate the level of casting technology in the Nanyue Kingdom at the time, but also provide important material evidence for the history of Guangzhou\u0027s founding as a city."},{"number":25,"translatedQuestion":"陈家祠又名陈氏书院，又是陈氏子弟读书求学的学堂。在建筑装饰艺术上，陈家祠集广东民间工艺之大成，被誉为＂岭南艺术建筑明珠＂，故1959年被辟为广东民间艺术博物馆。","translatedAnswer":"The Chen Clan Ancestral Hall, also known as the Chen Clan Academy, served as a school where members of the Chen clan studied. Its architectural decoration brings together the finest of Guangdong\u0027s folk crafts, earning it the title “a pearl of Lingnan architecture and art.” It was therefore converted into the Guangdong Folk Arts Museum in 1959."},{"number":26,"translatedQuestion":"正门外两边墙上的砖雕描绘了两个不同的历史故事，这些砖雕是广东砖雕艺术的代表作。","translatedAnswer":"The brick carvings on the walls to either side outside the main entrance depict two different historical stories. They are representative masterpieces of Guangdong\u0027s brick-carving art."},{"number":27,"translatedQuestion":"正门外两侧的石鼓为门第的象征。在封建旧中国，只有当某家族中有人在科举中取得进士以上的名次时才能在其宅第前安放大鼓。","translatedAnswer":"The stone drums on either side outside the main entrance symbolize the family\u0027s social standing. In feudal China, a family could place large drums in front of its residence only if one of its members had attained the rank of jinshi or higher in the imperial examinations."},{"number":28,"translatedQuestion":"大门上的人像为门神．最初的门神为神荼和郁垒，是传说中能制服恶鬼护卫宅第的神。他们身披盔甲，手拿绳索，丑怪凶恶。","translatedAnswer":"The figures on the gate are door gods. The earliest door gods were Shentu and Yulei, legendary deities who could subdue evil spirits and protect a household. They are shown wearing armor and holding ropes, with ugly and fearsome appearances."},{"number":29,"translatedQuestion":"请看这幅图案；一只母鸡和数只小鸡正在芭蕉树下悠闲自在地觅食。这些鸡其实比作现世中的两代人；而芭蕉树叶很大，＂大叶＂与＂大业＂同音，故芭蕉的大叶子比作先辈们所开创的＂大业＂。","translatedAnswer":"Please look at this design: a hen and several chicks are leisurely foraging beneath a banana plant. The chickens represent two generations of people in the present world. The plant\u0027s leaves are large, and “large leaf” (daye) is a homophone of “great undertaking” (daye); thus, the banana plant\u0027s broad leaves symbolize the great undertaking begun by the ancestors."},{"number":30,"translatedQuestion":"后堂是陈氏族人祭祀祖先的地方。这里的神龛上安放着陈氏祖先的牌位，正中最高者为陈氏远祖舜帝，后世列祖按辈分排列。","translatedAnswer":"The rear hall is where members of the Chen clan worship their ancestors. The shrine here holds the ancestral tablets of the Chen family. The highest tablet in the center is that of Emperor Shun, the clan\u0027s remote ancestor; the later generations of ancestors are arranged according to their respective generations."}]},"en-zh":{"category":"en-zh","items":[{"number":1,"translatedQuestion":"中山纪念堂于1931年10月竣工。它是一座八角形、宫殿式的钢筋混凝土建筑，高58米，建筑面积1.2万平方米。其外观仿若中国传统宫殿，建造时却采用了现代建筑技术。","translatedAnswer":"中山纪念堂于1931年10月建成，主体建筑高58米，建筑面积1.2万平方米。它是一座八角形的宫殿式钢筋混凝土建筑，外观具有中国传统建筑艺术风格，结构则采用了当时最新的建筑技术。"},{"number":2,"translatedQuestion":"孙中山从小便受到西方基督教思想和民主思想的影响，这促使他下定决心，革除旧中国封建社会的弊病，使中国成为一个民主、强大的国家。","translatedAnswer":"孙中山自幼受到西方民主思想的影响，立志把中国建设成为一个民主、富强的国家。"},{"number":3,"translatedQuestion":"1894年，孙中山在檀香山创立了中国第一个资产阶级革命组织——兴中会。次年春天，他回到香港，并在广州发动了反清的第一次武装起义，但以失败告终。","translatedAnswer":"1894年，孙中山在美国檀香山成立了中国最早的资产阶级革命团体——兴中会。次年春天，他在广州发动了旨在推翻清王朝的第一次武装起义，但起义最终失败。"},{"number":4,"translatedQuestion":"1905年，孙中山前往日本，创立了中国第一个政党——中国同盟会，后来该组织发展为国民党。此后，他接连尝试推翻清朝统治，最终在1911年10月爆发的武昌起义中取得成功。清朝统治随之被推翻，他当选为中华民国临时政府的临时大总统。","translatedAnswer":"1905年，孙中山在日本成立了中国第一个革命政党——同盟会（即国民党的前身），致力于推翻清朝的革命斗争。1911年武昌起义爆发后，革命取得成功，清朝统治被推翻，孙中山当选为中华民国临时大总统。"},{"number":5,"translatedQuestion":"中山纪念堂是一座可容纳3,238人的会堂。会堂的音响效果极佳，而且没有立柱遮挡观众视线：支撑巨大穹顶的四根大跨度钢桁架由八根承重柱支撑，这些柱子都隐藏在墙内。","translatedAnswer":"中山纪念堂是一座可容纳3,238名观众的大会堂。会堂内看不到支撑柱：整个大屋顶由四根大跨度钢桁架支撑，八根柱子巧妙地隐藏在墙内。观众无论坐在大厅的哪个位置，视线都不会被柱子遮挡；会堂内回声很小，音响效果极佳。"},{"number":6,"translatedQuestion":"中山纪念堂由一位名叫吕彦直的年轻中国建筑师设计。他出生于天津，毕业于北京清华大学，后来赴美国康奈尔大学学习建筑。纪念堂尚未建成时，他便于1929年因肺癌去世，年仅36岁。","translatedAnswer":"中山纪念堂由年轻的中国建筑师吕彦直先生设计。吕彦直出生于天津，毕业于北京清华大学，后赴美国康奈尔大学学习建筑。1929年，纪念堂尚未落成，他便因肺癌病逝，年仅36岁。"},{"number":7,"translatedQuestion":"开平碉楼是一种融合中西建筑风格的建筑，兼具家族居所和防匪瞭望楼的功能。鼎盛时期，这类建筑共有3,300座。如今，登记在册的有1,833座。国务院已将其列为国家级文物保护单位。","translatedAnswer":"开平碉楼集防卫、居住和中西建筑艺术于一体，是国务院公布的国家重点文物保护单位。鼎盛时期，开平碉楼有3,300多座，目前登记在册的有1,833座。"},{"number":8,"translatedQuestion":"开平碉楼形态各异、风格多样。从功能上看，有供一个家族居住的居楼，有几户人家共同建造、用作临时避难所的众楼，还有兼作坚固住宅和防匪瞭望楼的建筑。","translatedAnswer":"开平碉楼千姿百态、形式多样。从功能上看，主要分为三类：供家族居住的居楼、由村民共同集资建造的众楼，以及主要用于打更、放哨和防匪的更楼。"},{"number":9,"translatedQuestion":"开平最高、最豪华的碉楼是瑞石楼。这座九层楼高25米，以钢筋混凝土建成。楼内设施与陈设布局典雅，具有中国传统风格，因此被誉为开平第一楼。","translatedAnswer":"瑞石楼是开平最高、最豪华的碉楼，共九层，高25米，采用钢筋混凝土结构。楼内设施布局典雅，富有中国传统格调，因此被誉为开平第一楼。"},{"number":10,"translatedQuestion":"立园位于塘口镇北义村，建于1936年，是一座融合中西风格的花园别墅。高大的拱形门上方刻着“立园”二字，这就是园名。","translatedAnswer":"开平立园位于塘口镇北义乡，建于1936年，是一座融合中西园林风格与情调的花园别墅。高大的圆拱形门上题有“立园”二字。"},{"number":11,"translatedQuestion":"立园分为别墅区、大花园区和小花园区。主要建筑包括题有“立园”二字的拱门、刻有“修身立本”四字（大意为“修养自身，成为有道德的人”）的装饰牌坊、桥上的四座亭子，以及两座古罗马风格建筑——“鸟巢”和“藤亭”。","translatedAnswer":"立园分为别墅区、大花园区和小花园区。主要建筑有题着“立园”二字的大牌坊、刻有“修身立本”四字的大牌楼、四座桥亭，以及古罗马式建筑“鸟巢”和“花藤亭”。"},{"number":12,"translatedQuestion":"别墅区的住宅建筑也融合了中西建筑元素。其主体结构具有外国建筑风格，有些屋顶则仿照中国宫殿式样，室内装饰与陈设兼有中外特色。","translatedAnswer":"别墅区的住宅建筑也融合了中西建筑元素。其主体结构为西式风格，有些屋顶仿若中国宫殿，室内装修和家具则兼具中西特色。"},{"number":13,"translatedQuestion":"2500万年前，丹霞山所在之处还是一片广阔低洼的湖泊。后来，地壳运动使地面隆起，湖水退去，湖底原有的沉积物逐渐氧化，形成红色岩石，最终这里成为一座山。","translatedAnswer":"2500万年前，丹霞山所在之处原是一片低平的湖泊。后来，受地壳运动影响，地面上升，湖水退尽，形成高耸的山峰；原先湖底的沉积物经氧化后变成了红色岩石。"},{"number":14,"translatedQuestion":"除南极洲外，世界各大洲都能找到丹霞地貌，例如美国的大峡谷和德国萨克森州的一些地区。不过，丹霞山是此类地貌中规模最大、最美丽、最具典型性的地方。","translatedAnswer":"除南极洲外，世界各大洲均有丹霞地貌，例如美国的大峡谷和德国萨克森州的一些地区。而丹霞山是世界上此类地貌中面积最大、发育最典型、风景最优美的地区。"},{"number":15,"translatedQuestion":"据地质研究，这根石柱曾是悬崖的一部分。大自然将它从山体中分离出来并雕琢成如今的形状，至今已有30多万年。","translatedAnswer":"据专家考证，阳元石原是山体石壁的一部分。大自然鬼斧神工，将它从山体中分离出来并雕琢成如今的形状，至今已有30万年。"},{"number":16,"translatedQuestion":"丹霞山占地319平方公里，600多座红色山峰从葱郁林木间拔地而起，宛如一座遍布红宝石雕塑的大花园。因此，丹霞山也被称为“中国红宝石公园”，字面上亦可称作“中国红石公园”。1995年，国务院批准其成为地质自然保护区。","translatedAnswer":"丹霞山总面积319平方公里，600多座大小石峰宛如一座红宝石雕塑园，因此又称“中国红石公园”。1995年，国务院批准其为国家地质自然保护区。"},{"number":17,"translatedQuestion":"请看右边远处那座山。它像一位躺下入睡的少女：从右向左，头部、颈部、胸部和腹部的轮廓都清晰可辨。因此，这一景观被称为“睡美人”或“玉女拦江”。","translatedAnswer":"请大家往右看：那座山宛如一位入睡的少女，从右到左，头部、颈部、胸部和腹部的轮廓清晰可辨，因此这一景观被称为“睡美人”或“玉女拦江”。"},{"number":18,"translatedQuestion":"释迦牟尼殿内有一块形似龙的岩石，名为“变色龙”。它会像变色龙随环境改变颜色一样，随季节变色：春天呈浅绿色，夏天呈深绿色，秋天呈黄绿色，冬天呈棕黄色，因此得名。","translatedAnswer":"大雄宝殿内有一块形似龙的石壁，颜色会随春夏秋冬而变化：春天嫩绿，夏天深绿，秋天黄绿，冬天褐黄，因此被称为“变色龙”。"},{"number":19,"translatedQuestion":"赵眜墓于1983年在广州解放北路的象岗山下被发现，墓室位于地下20米处。墓葬面积约100平方米，由750块红砂岩石砌成，分为前后两部分，共有七个墓室。","translatedAnswer":"南越王墓于1983年发现，位于广州解放北路象岗山上，墓室深入地下20米。陵墓面积约100平方米，由750多块红色砂岩石砌成，分前后两部分，共有七个墓室。"},{"number":20,"translatedQuestion":"南越王墓是迄今在中国南方发现的最重要的汉墓：墓葬规模最大，墓主人身份地位最高，出土文物数量也最多，因此被视为中国近代五大考古发现之一。","translatedAnswer":"南越文王墓是迄今为止在岭南地区发现的最重要的一座汉墓，其规模最大、出土文物最丰富、墓主人身份地位最高，因此被誉为中国近代考古五大新发现之一。"},{"number":21,"translatedQuestion":"据史书记载，帝王印玺据说以白玉制成，印钮为虎形；而赵眜的这方印玺则以黄金制成，印钮为龙形。它由南越国铸造，是赵眜生前实际使用的印玺。","translatedAnswer":"文献记载，帝印为白玉质、螭虎钮；赵眜这枚帝印则为金质、蟠龙钮，是南越国自铸、赵眜生前实际使用的印章。"},{"number":22,"translatedQuestion":"其中尤其值得一提的是一套青铜编钟，名为“句鑃”，共八件。钟上铸有“文帝九年乐府工造”的铭文，表明这套乐器于公元前129年在南越国铸造。尽管已有2100多年历史，它们至今仍能奏出清晰、准确的音调，令人惊叹。","translatedAnswer":"其中尤其值得一提的是一套句鑃，共八件。青铜编钟表面铸有“文帝九年乐府工造”的铭文，表明这套乐器铸造于公元前129年。历经2100多年，音质依然良好，至今仍能演奏，弥足珍贵。"},{"number":23,"translatedQuestion":"从其造型和装饰纹样来看，这件器物与中国传统银器迥然不同。考古学家结合药丸的化学分析认为，它是从海外波斯输入的制品，盒内装的是一种阿拉伯药物。","translatedAnswer":"从造型和纹饰工艺来看，这件银盒与中国传统银器风格迥异。经药丸化学分析及专家研究，考古学家认为它是波斯制品，盒中药丸很可能是阿拉伯药物，因此这件银盒应为海外舶来品。"},{"number":24,"translatedQuestion":"这件玉衣长1.73米，由2,291片玉片制成，以红丝线串缀而成，形成了色彩鲜明、优美多样的图案。","translatedAnswer":"整件玉衣长1.73米，共用玉片2,291块，以朱红丝线串缀，组成多重图案，色彩鲜艳夺目。"},{"number":25,"translatedQuestion":"陈家祠又称陈氏书院，也曾是陈氏家族子弟读书求学的学堂。1959年，它被改建为广州民间艺术博物馆，因为祠堂建筑本身全面展现了精湛的广东民间工艺。","translatedAnswer":"陈家祠又名陈氏书院，曾是陈氏子弟读书求学的学堂。其建筑装饰艺术集广东民间工艺之大成，被誉为“岭南艺术建筑明珠”，因此于1959年改建为广东民间艺术博物馆。"},{"number":26,"translatedQuestion":"正门入口处的石鼓象征着陈氏家族的社会地位。在清代封建社会，只有家族中有人在科举考试中考取进士（或获得更高功名），才可以在宅第前摆放一对石鼓。","translatedAnswer":"正门外两侧的石鼓象征着家族门第。在中国封建社会，只有家族中有人在科举考试中取得进士及以上功名，才可以在宅第前安放一对石鼓。"},{"number":27,"translatedQuestion":"门扇上的两幅画像是门神。最初的门神是传说中的神荼和郁垒，据说他们能够捉鬼、驱邪并守护宅院。他们身穿华丽盔甲，手持绳索，模样威严可畏。","translatedAnswer":"大门上的人物画像是门神。最初的门神是传说中的神荼和郁垒，据说他们能制服恶鬼、护卫宅第。他们身披盔甲，手持绳索，形象威严可畏。"},{"number":28,"translatedQuestion":"栏杆柱顶端雕刻着几种盛产于华南的水果，如桃子、杨桃、木瓜和荔枝等。这些雕刻是供奉给陈氏祖先的象征性供品。","translatedAnswer":"平台栏杆柱的顶端分别雕有多种岭南佳果，如蟠桃、杨桃、木瓜、荔枝等，象征陈氏族人供奉给祖先的供品。"},{"number":29,"translatedQuestion":"祠堂后殿是陈氏族人祭祀祖先的地方。神龛上过去供奉着祖先牌位，并按家族辈分排列。最高处的牌位属于“舜帝”，陈氏族人相信他是本族的远祖。","translatedAnswer":"后堂是陈氏族人祭祀祖先的地方。神龛上曾供奉陈氏祖先的牌位，按家族辈分排列；位于最高处的是陈氏远祖舜帝。"},{"number":30,"translatedQuestion":"这幅图意在说明，年轻人学得快，但要成为学识渊博的人，需要多年积累；它也可能是在阐释英语谚语“罗马不是一天建成的”。这幅图旨在鼓励人们持之以恒地学习。","translatedAnswer":"此图寓意年轻人学习进步很快，但要在学业上有所成就并非一朝一夕之功，可能要到晚年才能成器。"}]}}};
